% 32e-ir-assign-call.weft — IR lowering of assignment, calls, and tail expressions
%
% Split from 32-ir-builder.weft (structural refactor, no content changes):
% assignment (incl. the address-lvalue helper), function calls, variadic
% builtins, string literals, the conditional operator, and casts.

\subsection{Assignment}

Assignment handles three cases.  A simple \texttt{=} evaluates the
RHS, looks up the LHS identifier in the locals table, and emits
\texttt{IR\_STORE} to write the value into the variable's alloca.
Compound assignments (\texttt{+=}, \texttt{-=}, \texttt{*=},
\texttt{/=}, \texttt{\%=}, \texttt{\&=}, \texttt{|=}, \texttt{\^{}=},
\texttt{<<=}, \texttt{>>=}) first load the current value, emit the
corresponding binary operation, then store the result back---exactly
the read-modify-write pattern described for increment/decrement above.
Finally, if the LHS identifier is not in locals, it must be a global:
the builder emits \texttt{IR\_GLOBAL\_REF} to materialise the
address.  For simple assignment the value is stored directly; for
compound operators the same load-binop-store sequence is applied,
loading from the global address vreg before emitting the binary
operation and storing the result back.  That reload must carry the
lvalue type's access-size code (\texttt{IR\_SRC2}), exactly as the
local-alloca path and the matching store do: a narrow scalar occupies
only the low bytes of its natural width, so a hand-built
\texttt{IR\_LOAD} that left the size code zero would read a raw 64-bit
word and splice adjacent memory (or, for a signed global, fail to
sign-extend) into the value.  A subsequent arithmetic \texttt{>>=} then
shifted that un-extended pattern, turning an arithmetic right shift into
a logical one---so \texttt{int g; g >>= 4} with \texttt{g < 0} produced
a large positive.  Routing the reload through
\texttt{\_kcc\_ir\_emit\_load\_t} sets both the size code and the type,
keeping the global path identical to the local one.

The three \emph{address-based} lvalues---a member \texttt{s.f}, a
subscript \texttt{a[i]}, and a dereference \texttt{*p}---share a single
helper, \texttt{kcc\_ir\_assign\_through}.  Each computes its target
address once, then hands the address, the right-hand value, the operator
token, an optional bit-field descriptor, and the access type to the
helper, which performs a plain store for \texttt{=} or a
load-operate-store for a compound operator, sized to the lvalue's type
(so a narrow field is not clobbered) and routed through the bit-field
read-modify-write when the target is a bit-field.  Sharing this path is
what makes \texttt{s.f\;+=\;v}, \texttt{a[i]\;*=\;v}, and
\texttt{*p\;-=\;v} work uniformly---and, because the helper reuses the
bit-field primitives, a compound assignment to a bit-field member folds
out for free.

When the LHS has an aggregate type---\texttt{TY\_STRUCT} \emph{or}
\texttt{TY\_UNION}---a single store would only copy the first eight bytes.
A union is easy to overlook here: syntactically \texttt{*dst = *src} looks
like a scalar assignment, and if the union is not recognised the store
copies one word and leaves the rest of the object stale.  This is exactly
how Lua's hash table corrupted itself: \texttt{luaH\_newkey} moves a
colliding \texttt{Node} (a union) with \texttt{*f = *mp}, and a one-word
copy left the moved node's \texttt{next} link garbage, so the collision
chain no longer reached its own main position and \texttt{luaH\_newkey}
spun forever walking it.  So both aggregate kinds take the copy path.

The builder resolves both the LHS and RHS base addresses (alloca vreg or
global ref) and calls \texttt{\_kcc\_ir\_emit\_struct\_copy}, which copies
exactly \texttt{TY\_SIZE} bytes---\emph{not} a number of fields, which for a
union (a handful of overlapping members) or a struct with sub-word fields
bears no relation to its size, and copying the wrong amount either truncates
the object or runs off its end into the neighbour.  Both sides accept the same lvalue
shapes: a plain variable, an aggregate-typed member, an array element
\texttt{a[i]}, or a dereferenced pointer \texttt{*p}, whose pointer
\emph{value} is the aggregate's address.  The destination must recognise
each of these just as the source does---otherwise the copy is silently
dropped, leaving the target uninitialised.  The \emph{source} accepts three
more shapes that carry an aggregate value without naming an lvalue directly:
a parenthesised expression; a comma operator whose right operand is the
aggregate (\texttt{g = (setup(), s)}); and a nested assignment
(\texttt{a = b = c}), whose value is the object it just assigned.  The builder
peels the parentheses, evaluates a comma's discarded left side for its side
effects, and \emph{builds} a nested assignment---performing the inner copy---then
takes the address it returns as the source.  Matching no case for these is what
let \texttt{g = (1, other)} and \texttt{a = b = c} type-check yet copy nothing.

The compound path uses callee-saved registers \texttt{x23} (current
value vreg) and \texttt{x24} (IR opcode), so the frame must be 64
bytes to save \texttt{x19}--\texttt{x24}.  In all cases the returned
vreg is the stored value, matching the C semantics where assignment
is an expression (\stdref{6.5.16}).

The current-value load that opens the compound path must carry the same
narrow access-size code (from \texttt{\_kcc\_ir\_load\_width}) that its
paired store uses---not the natural 8-byte default.  A narrow scalar
occupies only the low bytes of its 8-byte stack slot, and the store writes
just those bytes, so an 8-byte reload would splice the slot's stale upper
bytes into the value.  This was invisible while the leaked bits happened to
be zero, but surfaced in zlib's \texttt{inflate\_table}: an
\texttt{unsigned incr} written 4 bytes wide and then updated with
\texttt{incr >>= 1} was reloaded 8 bytes wide, so the 64-bit shift dragged
the garbage upper word down into \texttt{incr} the first time the loop body
ran, corrupting the Huffman sub-table walk into an out-of-bounds write.

Parentheses around the target are transparent: \texttt{(x) = v} is
\texttt{x = v}.  The lvalue dispatch keys on the target's node tag
(identifier, member, subscript, dereference); a stray
\texttt{AST\_PAREN\_EXPR} matches none of them and the store is silently
dropped.  This is not a rare corner: the macro
\texttt{\#define G(L) (L->l\_G)} makes \texttt{G(L) = g} expand to
\texttt{(L->l\_G) = g}, so \texttt{preinit\_thread}'s wiring of the
global-state pointer vanished, leaving \texttt{L->l\_G} null and crashing
the first allocation through it.  So we peel any parenthesis wrappers off
the target first, rewriting the assignment node's LHS field to the bare
lvalue before dispatching.

@d IR: build assign @{
// -- kcc_ir_build_assign ---
// uint64_t kcc_ir_build_assign(node *expr)
//   x0 = AST_ASSIGN_EXPR (F0=op, F1=lhs, F2=rhs)
// For simple assignment (=), evaluates RHS and stores to LHS alloca.
.text
.globl _kcc_ir_build_assign
.p2align 2
_kcc_ir_build_assign:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-80]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    stp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    stp x23, x24, [sp, #48]

    mov x19, x0

    // Parentheses are transparent to an assignment target: `(x) = v` is
    // `x = v`.  Peel any AST_PAREN_EXPR off the LHS and rewrite it in place,
    // so the lvalue dispatch below sees the real target instead of dropping
    // the store (as `G(L) = g`, i.e. `(L->l_G) = g`, otherwise would).
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F1]      // LHS node
.Liba_unparen:
    cbz x0, .Liba_unparen_done
    ldr x1, [x0, #AST_TAG]
    cmp x1, #AST_PAREN_EXPR
    b.ne .Liba_unparen_done
    ldr x0, [x0, #AST_F0]       // inner expression
    str x0, [x19, #AST_F1]      // rewrite LHS → inner (parens dropped)
    b .Liba_unparen
.Liba_unparen_done:

    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F3]      // assignment result type (= LHS type)
    str x0, [sp, #64]           // stashed for compound-assign width normalisation
    ldr x20, [x19, #AST_F0]     // op token

    // Check for struct assignment (simple assign only)
    cmp x20, #TOK_ASSIGN
    b.ne .Liba_normal_rhs
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F1]      // LHS node
    cbz x0, .Liba_normal_rhs
    ldr x1, [x0, #AST_F3]       // LHS sema type
    cbz x1, .Liba_normal_rhs
    ldr x2, [x1, #TY_KIND]
    cmp x2, #TY_STRUCT
    b.eq .Liba_struct_copy
    cmp x2, #TY_UNION
    b.eq .Liba_struct_copy

.Liba_normal_rhs:
    // Build RHS first
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F2]
    ldr x2, [x0, #AST_F3]       // rhs sema type
    str x2, [sp, #72]
    bl _kcc_ir_build_expr
    mov x21, x0                  // rhs vreg

    // Simple assignment converts the RHS to the LHS type across the
    // int/float boundary ([sp,#64] holds the assignment's (LHS) type).
    cmp x20, #TOK_ASSIGN
    b.ne .Liba_get_lhs
    mov x0, x21
    ldr x1, [sp, #72]           // from = rhs type
    ldr x2, [sp, #64]           // to   = LHS type
    bl _kcc_ir_convert
    mov x21, x0

.Liba_get_lhs:
    // Get LHS — identifier, subscript, member, or dereference
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F1]
    cbz x0, .Liba_done
    ldr x1, [x0, #AST_TAG]
    cmp x1, #AST_SUBSCRIPT
    b.eq .Liba_subscript
    cmp x1, #AST_MEMBER
    b.eq .Liba_member
    cmp x1, #AST_UNARY_EXPR
    b.eq .Liba_deref
    cmp x1, #AST_IDENT_EXPR
    b.ne .Liba_done

    // Look up variable alloca
    ldr x22, [x0, #AST_F0]      // name ptr
    cbz x22, .Liba_done

    mov x0, x22
    bl _kcc_strlen
    mov x2, x0

    adrp x0, _kcc_ir_locals@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_ir_locals@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x0, [x0]
    mov x1, x22
    bl _kcc_ht_get
    cbz x0, .Liba_global_store
    bic x0, x0, #0x8000000000000000
    sub x22, x0, #1              // alloca vreg (stored as vreg+1)

    // For compound assignment (+=, etc.), load current value first
    cmp x20, #TOK_ASSIGN
    b.eq .Liba_store

    // Load current value.  Use the lvalue's own type (stashed at [sp,#64]) so a
    // floating local's compound update stays floating: the binop below inherits
    // this type and the back end selects FADD/FSUB/FMUL/FDIV instead of the
    // integer opcodes.  The load also carries an access-size code (IR_SRC2)
    // derived from that type, exactly as the matching store does below: a
    // narrow scalar occupies only the low bytes of its 8-byte slot, so reading
    // it back at the natural 8-byte width would splice the slot's stale upper
    // bytes into the value.  For an `unsigned incr` written with a 4-byte store,
    // an 8-byte reload followed by `incr >>= 1` would shift that garbage down.
    mov x0, #IR_LOAD
    bl _kcc_ir_alloc_instr
    ldr x1, [sp, #64]           // lvalue (LHS) type
    str x1, [x0, #IR_TYPE]
    str x22, [x0, #IR_SRC1]
    mov x19, x0
    ldr x0, [sp, #64]           // lvalue type → load access-size code (IR_SRC2)
    bl _kcc_ir_load_width
    str x0, [x19, #IR_SRC2]     // narrow read must zero/sign-extend, not slurp 8 bytes
    bl _kcc_ir_next_vreg
    str x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    mov x23, x0                  // current value vreg
    mov x0, x19
    bl _kcc_ir_emit

    // Map compound op to IR opcode
    mov x24, #IR_ADD             // default
    cmp x20, #TOK_ADD_ASSIGN
    b.eq .Liba_compound
    mov x24, #IR_SUB
    cmp x20, #TOK_SUB_ASSIGN
    b.eq .Liba_compound
    mov x24, #IR_MUL
    cmp x20, #TOK_MUL_ASSIGN
    b.eq .Liba_compound
    mov x24, #IR_SDIV
    cmp x20, #TOK_DIV_ASSIGN
    b.eq .Liba_compound
    mov x24, #IR_SMOD
    cmp x20, #TOK_MOD_ASSIGN
    b.eq .Liba_compound
    mov x24, #IR_AND
    cmp x20, #TOK_AND_ASSIGN
    b.eq .Liba_compound
    mov x24, #IR_OR
    cmp x20, #TOK_OR_ASSIGN
    b.eq .Liba_compound
    mov x24, #IR_XOR
    cmp x20, #TOK_XOR_ASSIGN
    b.eq .Liba_compound
    mov x24, #IR_SHL
    cmp x20, #TOK_SHL_ASSIGN
    b.eq .Liba_compound
    mov x24, #IR_ASHR
    cmp x20, #TOK_SHR_ASSIGN
    b.eq .Liba_compound
    // Fallthrough: treat as simple assign
    b .Liba_store

.Liba_compound:
    // Apply the compound operation under the usual arithmetic conversions: the
    // shared helper computes in the common type (floating when either operand
    // is) and converts the result back to the lvalue type.
    mov x0, x24                  // op (IR opcode)
    mov x1, x23                  // old value
    ldr x2, [sp, #64]           // L (lvalue type)
    mov x3, x21                  // rhs value
    ldr x4, [sp, #72]           // R (rhs type)
    bl _kcc_ir_compound_apply
    mov x21, x0                  // value to store

.Liba_store:
    // Emit store.  The access-size code comes from the lvalue type (stashed at
    // [sp,#64]) so a narrow scalar---especially a `float`, whose 4-byte single
    // representation differs from the double working format---is written at its
    // true width rather than as a 64-bit default.
    mov x0, #IR_STORE
    bl _kcc_ir_alloc_instr
    mov x23, x0                  // store instr
    adrp x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x1, [x1]
    str x1, [x23, #IR_TYPE]
    str x21, [x23, #IR_SRC1]     // value
    str x22, [x23, #IR_SRC2]     // alloca
    ldr x0, [sp, #64]           // lvalue type → access-size code
    bl _kcc_ir_store_width
    str x0, [x23, #IR_DEST]
    mov x0, x23
    bl _kcc_ir_emit

    mov x0, x21                  // return the stored value's vreg
    b .Liba_done

.Liba_global_store:
    // x22 = name ptr, x21 = rhs vreg, x20 = op token
    // static local? redirect the store onto its mangled global
    adrp x0, _kcc_ir_statics@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_ir_statics@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x0, [x0]
    cbz x0, .Liba_global_ref_emit
    mov x23, x0                  // ht
    mov x0, x22
    bl _kcc_strlen
    mov x2, x0                   // keylen
    mov x0, x23
    mov x1, x22                  // key = source name
    bl _kcc_ht_get
    cbz x0, .Liba_global_ref_emit
    mov x22, x0                  // x22 = mangled name

.Liba_global_ref_emit:
    // Emit global_ref to get address vreg
    mov x0, #IR_GLOBAL_REF
    bl _kcc_ir_alloc_instr
    str x22, [x0, #IR_SRC1]     // name ptr
    adrp x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x1, [x1]
    str x1, [x0, #IR_TYPE]
    mov x19, x0
    bl _kcc_ir_next_vreg
    str x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    mov x22, x0                  // x22 = global addr vreg
    mov x0, x19
    bl _kcc_ir_emit

    // For compound assignment on globals, load-modify-store
    cmp x20, #TOK_ASSIGN
    b.eq .Liba_global_simple

    // Load current value from global at the lvalue type's access width, so a
    // narrow signed global is sign-extended (ldrsw), not read as a raw 64-bit
    // word.  A hand-built IR_LOAD left IR_SRC2 (the access-size/sign code)
    // zero, which codegen lowers to a 64-bit `ldr`; feeding that
    // un-sign-extended value to a compound `>>=` made the arithmetic right
    // shift behave like a logical one (`int g >>= 4` with g<0 yielded a huge
    // positive).  Route through the shared load helper so the size code and
    // lvalue type (floating globals stay floating) are both set.
    ldr x0, [sp, #64]           // lvalue (LHS) type
    bl _kcc_ir_load_width       // x0 = access-size/sign code
    mov x1, x0                  // size code
    mov x0, x22                 // global addr vreg
    ldr x2, [sp, #64]           // lvalue type
    bl _kcc_ir_emit_load_t
    mov x23, x0                  // x23 = current value vreg

    // Map compound op to IR opcode (same table as local path)
    mov x24, #IR_ADD
    cmp x20, #TOK_ADD_ASSIGN
    b.eq .Liba_global_compound
    mov x24, #IR_SUB
    cmp x20, #TOK_SUB_ASSIGN
    b.eq .Liba_global_compound
    mov x24, #IR_MUL
    cmp x20, #TOK_MUL_ASSIGN
    b.eq .Liba_global_compound
    mov x24, #IR_SDIV
    cmp x20, #TOK_DIV_ASSIGN
    b.eq .Liba_global_compound
    mov x24, #IR_SMOD
    cmp x20, #TOK_MOD_ASSIGN
    b.eq .Liba_global_compound
    mov x24, #IR_AND
    cmp x20, #TOK_AND_ASSIGN
    b.eq .Liba_global_compound
    mov x24, #IR_OR
    cmp x20, #TOK_OR_ASSIGN
    b.eq .Liba_global_compound
    mov x24, #IR_XOR
    cmp x20, #TOK_XOR_ASSIGN
    b.eq .Liba_global_compound
    mov x24, #IR_SHL
    cmp x20, #TOK_SHL_ASSIGN
    b.eq .Liba_global_compound
    mov x24, #IR_ASHR
    cmp x20, #TOK_SHR_ASSIGN
    b.eq .Liba_global_compound
    b .Liba_global_simple

.Liba_global_compound:
    // Same shared helper as the local path: compute under the usual arithmetic
    // conversions and convert the result back to the lvalue type.
    mov x0, x24                  // op (IR opcode)
    mov x1, x23                  // current value
    ldr x2, [sp, #64]           // L (lvalue type)
    mov x3, x21                  // rhs
    ldr x4, [sp, #72]           // R (rhs type)
    bl _kcc_ir_compound_apply
    mov x21, x0                  // value to store

.Liba_global_simple:
    // Store value to global address, at the lvalue type's access width (so a
    // global `float` is written as a 4-byte single, not a 64-bit double).
    mov x0, #IR_STORE
    bl _kcc_ir_alloc_instr
    mov x23, x0                  // store instr
    adrp x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x1, [x1]
    str x1, [x23, #IR_TYPE]
    str x21, [x23, #IR_SRC1]     // value (rhs or compound result)
    str x22, [x23, #IR_SRC2]     // global addr
    ldr x0, [sp, #64]           // lvalue type → access-size code
    bl _kcc_ir_store_width
    str x0, [x23, #IR_DEST]
    mov x0, x23
    bl _kcc_ir_emit
    mov x0, x21
    b .Liba_done

.Liba_subscript:
    // LHS is a[i]: x0 = AST_SUBSCRIPT node, x21 = rhs vreg, x20 = op token.
    // Same load-op-store path as a member, so a[i] += v works too.
    mov x23, x0                  // subscript node
    bl _kcc_ir_build_subscript_addr
    mov x22, x0                  // element address
    mov x0, x22                  // addr
    mov x1, x21                  // rhs
    mov x2, x20                  // op token
    mov x3, #0                   // not a bit-field
    ldr x4, [x23, #AST_F3]      // element type → access width (lvalue type L)
    ldr x5, [sp, #72]           // rhs type R (for compound conversions)
    bl _kcc_ir_assign_through
    b .Liba_done

.Liba_member:
    // LHS is s.x: x0 = AST_MEMBER node, x21 = rhs vreg, x20 = op token.
    // The unified helper does simple or compound (load-op-store), and a
    // bit-field read-modify-write when the field carries bit metadata.
    mov x23, x0                  // member node
    bl _kcc_ir_build_member_addr
    mov x22, x0                  // field address
    adrp x0, _kcc_ir_member_field@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_ir_member_field@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x24, [x0]                // matched field decl (0 if none)
    mov x0, x22                  // addr
    mov x1, x21                  // rhs
    mov x2, x20                  // op token
    mov x3, x24                  // field (bit-field metadata)
    ldr x4, [x23, #AST_F3]      // member's resolved type → access width (L)
    ldr x5, [sp, #72]           // rhs type R (for compound conversions)
    bl _kcc_ir_assign_through
    b .Liba_done

.Liba_deref:
    // LHS is *expr — evaluate the pointer, store RHS through it (load-op-store
    // for compound), reusing the shared address-lvalue helper.
    ldr x0, [x0, #AST_F0]       // check op is TOK_STAR
    cmp x0, #TOK_STAR
    b.ne .Liba_done
    ldr x23, [x19, #AST_F1]     // unary *expr
    ldr x23, [x23, #AST_F3]     // sema type of *expr (may be 0 → 64-bit)
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F1]      // unary expr
    ldr x0, [x0, #AST_F1]       // operand of * (e.g., IdentExpr 'p')
    bl _kcc_ir_build_expr
    mov x22, x0                  // pointer vreg
    mov x0, x22                  // addr
    mov x1, x21                  // rhs
    mov x2, x20                  // op token
    mov x3, #0                   // not a bit-field
    mov x4, x23                  // pointed-to type → access width (lvalue type L)
    ldr x5, [sp, #72]           // rhs type R (for compound conversions)
    bl _kcc_ir_assign_through
    b .Liba_done

.Liba_struct_copy:
    // x19 = assign node, LHS type is TY_STRUCT
    // x0 = LHS node (from check above), x1 = struct type
    mov x21, x1                  // x21 = struct type

    // Get LHS base address: a plain identifier's alloca, or---when the
    // destination is a struct-typed member (`s.m = rhs`)---the member's own
    // address.  Without the member case a struct assignment to a member emitted
    // no copy at all, leaving the member uninitialised.
    ldr x1, [x0, #AST_TAG]
    cmp x1, #AST_MEMBER
    b.eq .Liba_sc_dst_member
    cmp x1, #AST_SUBSCRIPT
    b.eq .Liba_sc_dst_subscript
    cmp x1, #AST_UNARY_EXPR
    b.eq .Liba_sc_dst_deref
    cmp x1, #AST_IDENT_EXPR
    b.ne .Liba_done

    ldr x22, [x0, #AST_F0]      // LHS name
    mov x0, x22
    bl _kcc_strlen
    mov x2, x0
    adrp x0, _kcc_ir_locals@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_ir_locals@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x0, [x0]
    mov x1, x22
    bl _kcc_ht_get
    cbz x0, .Liba_sc_dst_global

    bic x0, x0, #0x8000000000000000
    sub x23, x0, #1              // x23 = dst alloca vreg
    b .Liba_sc_get_src

.Liba_sc_dst_global:
    mov x0, #IR_GLOBAL_REF
    bl _kcc_ir_alloc_instr
    str x22, [x0, #IR_SRC1]
    adrp x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x1, [x1]
    str x1, [x0, #IR_TYPE]
    mov x22, x0
    bl _kcc_ir_next_vreg
    str x0, [x22, #IR_DEST]
    mov x23, x0                  // x23 = dst global ref vreg
    mov x0, x22
    bl _kcc_ir_emit
    b .Liba_sc_get_src

.Liba_sc_dst_member:
    // LHS is a struct-typed member: its GEP address is the copy destination.
    bl _kcc_ir_build_member_addr
    mov x23, x0                  // x23 = dst member address vreg
    b .Liba_sc_get_src

.Liba_sc_dst_subscript:
    // LHS is an array element `a[i]`: its computed address is the destination.
    bl _kcc_ir_build_subscript_addr
    mov x23, x0                  // x23 = dst element address vreg
    b .Liba_sc_get_src

.Liba_sc_dst_deref:
    // LHS is `*p` (pointer to a struct): the pointer's value IS the destination
    // struct's address, so building the pointer operand yields the dst base.
    ldr x1, [x0, #AST_F0]       // unary op
    cmp x1, #TOK_STAR
    b.ne .Liba_done
    ldr x0, [x0, #AST_F1]       // pointer operand
    bl _kcc_ir_build_expr
    mov x23, x0                  // x23 = dst base address vreg

.Liba_sc_get_src:
    // Get RHS base address: an identifier's alloca, or a struct-returning
    // call's result buffer.
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F2]
    cbz x0, .Liba_done
.Liba_sc_unwrap:
    // Peel parentheses, and evaluate the discarded left side of any comma
    // operator, before reading the RHS's real shape.  A struct assignment whose
    // right side is `(x , s)` still copies the struct `s`; without this the comma
    // (and paren) node matched no case below and the copy was silently skipped
    // --- `g = (1, other)` left `g` unchanged.  x20 holds the node across the
    // side-effect build (it is callee-saved, so build_expr preserves it).
    ldr x1, [x0, #AST_TAG]
    cmp x1, #AST_PAREN_EXPR
    b.ne .Liba_sc_chkcomma
    ldr x0, [x0, #AST_F0]       // unwrap parentheses
    cbz x0, .Liba_done
    b .Liba_sc_unwrap
.Liba_sc_chkcomma:
    cmp x1, #AST_COMMA_EXPR
    b.ne .Liba_sc_shape
    mov x20, x0                 // save the comma node
    ldr x0, [x20, #AST_F0]      // comma LHS: evaluate for side effects, discard
    bl _kcc_ir_build_expr
    ldr x0, [x20, #AST_F1]      // comma RHS is the struct source
    cbz x0, .Liba_done
    b .Liba_sc_unwrap
.Liba_sc_shape:
    ldr x1, [x0, #AST_TAG]
    cmp x1, #AST_ASSIGN_EXPR
    b.eq .Liba_sc_src_assign
    cmp x1, #AST_CALL_EXPR
    b.eq .Liba_sc_src_call
    cmp x1, #AST_MEMBER
    b.eq .Liba_sc_src_member
    cmp x1, #AST_SUBSCRIPT
    b.eq .Liba_sc_src_subscript
    cmp x1, #AST_UNARY_EXPR
    b.eq .Liba_sc_src_deref
    cmp x1, #AST_VA_ARG
    b.eq .Liba_sc_src_va_arg
    cmp x1, #AST_IDENT_EXPR
    b.ne .Liba_done

    ldr x22, [x0, #AST_F0]      // RHS name
    mov x0, x22
    bl _kcc_strlen
    mov x2, x0
    adrp x0, _kcc_ir_locals@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_ir_locals@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x0, [x0]
    mov x1, x22
    bl _kcc_ht_get
    cbz x0, .Liba_sc_src_global

    bic x0, x0, #0x8000000000000000
    sub x24, x0, #1              // x24 = src alloca vreg
    b .Liba_sc_do_copy

.Liba_sc_src_global:
    mov x0, #IR_GLOBAL_REF
    bl _kcc_ir_alloc_instr
    str x22, [x0, #IR_SRC1]
    adrp x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x1, [x1]
    str x1, [x0, #IR_TYPE]
    mov x22, x0
    bl _kcc_ir_next_vreg
    str x0, [x22, #IR_DEST]
    mov x24, x0                  // x24 = src global ref vreg
    mov x0, x22
    bl _kcc_ir_emit
    b .Liba_sc_do_copy

.Liba_sc_src_member:
    // RHS is a struct-typed member (`dst = a.m`): its GEP address is the source.
    bl _kcc_ir_build_member_addr
    mov x24, x0
    b .Liba_sc_do_copy

.Liba_sc_src_subscript:
    // RHS is an array element (`dst = a[i]`): its computed address is the source.
    bl _kcc_ir_build_subscript_addr
    mov x24, x0
    b .Liba_sc_do_copy

.Liba_sc_src_deref:
    // RHS is `*p` (a dereferenced pointer to a struct): the pointer's value IS
    // the source struct's address, so building the pointer operand suffices.
    ldr x1, [x0, #AST_F0]       // unary op
    cmp x1, #TOK_STAR
    b.ne .Liba_done
    ldr x0, [x0, #AST_F1]       // pointer operand
    bl _kcc_ir_build_expr
    mov x24, x0
    b .Liba_sc_do_copy

.Liba_sc_src_assign:
    // RHS is a nested assignment `dst2 = src2` (a chain `a = b = c`): build it,
    // performing the inner aggregate copy, then use its result --- the address
    // of the just-assigned object --- as this copy's source.  A struct
    // assignment returns that address, so `a = (b = c)` copies c into b and then
    // b into a; without this the chained copy matched no case and did nothing.
    bl _kcc_ir_build_assign
    mov x24, x0                   // x24 = assigned object's address vreg
    b .Liba_sc_do_copy

.Liba_sc_src_call:
    // RHS is a struct-returning call: its value is the result buffer address.
    bl _kcc_ir_build_call
    mov x24, x0                   // x24 = src base vreg
    b .Liba_sc_do_copy

.Liba_sc_src_va_arg:
    // RHS is `va_arg(ap, struct T)`: build_va_arg yields the argument object's
    // address (and advances the cursor), which is the copy source.
    bl _kcc_ir_build_va_arg
    mov x24, x0                   // x24 = src base vreg
    b .Liba_sc_do_copy

.Liba_sc_do_copy:
    // x24 = src, x23 = dst, x21 = aggregate type.  Copy exactly TY_SIZE bytes;
    // a field count would be wrong for a union or a struct with sub-word fields.
    // _kcc_type_layout is idempotent, so it just returns the (already-computed)
    // byte size.
    mov x0, x21
    bl _kcc_type_layout         // x0 = aggregate byte size
    mov x2, x0                    // byte size (exact)
    mov x0, x24                  // src
    mov x1, x23                  // dst
    bl _kcc_ir_emit_struct_copy
    mov x0, x23
    b .Liba_done

.Liba_done:
    ldp x23, x24, [sp, #48]
    ldp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #80
    ret
@| _kcc_ir_build_assign
@}

\paragraph{End-to-end tests.}
Compound assignment operators follow the read-modify-write pattern for
both local and global variables.  For locals, the builder loads via
the alloca vreg; for globals, it emits \texttt{IR\_GLOBAL\_REF} to
materialise the address, then the same load-binop-store sequence.  The
final three tests specifically exercise the global path, which was
initially missing and caused the RHS to overwrite the variable without
reading its current value.

@d Test: codegen compound @{
echo ""
echo "--- Compound Assignment ---"

run_test "add-assign" \
    "int main(void) { int x = 10; x += 5; return x; }" 15

run_test "sub-assign" \
    "int main(void) { int x = 10; x -= 5; return x; }" 5

run_test "mul-assign" \
    "int main(void) { int x = 10; x *= 5; return x; }" 50

run_test "div-assign" \
    "int main(void) { int x = 100; x /= 4; return x; }" 25

run_test "mod-assign" \
    "int main(void) { int x = 17; x %= 5; return x; }" 2

run_test "and-assign" \
    "int main(void) { int x = 0xFF; x &= 0x0F; return x; }" 15

run_test "or-assign" \
    "int main(void) { int x = 0xF0; x |= 0x0F; return x; }" 255

run_test "xor-assign" \
    "int main(void) { int x = 0xFF; x ^= 0x0F; return x; }" 240

run_test "shl-assign" \
    "int main(void) { int x = 1; x <<= 4; return x; }" 16

run_test "shr-assign" \
    "int main(void) { int x = 32; x >>= 2; return x; }" 8

run_test "compound in loop" \
    "int main(void) { int s = 0; for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) s += i; return s; }" 15

run_test "chained compound" \
    "int main(void) { int a = 1; int b = 2; a += 10; b *= 3; return a + b; }" 17

run_test "global add-assign" \
    "int g; int main(void) { g = 10; g += 5; return g; }" 15

run_test "global mul-assign" \
    "int g; int main(void) { g = 6; g *= 7; return g; }" 42

run_test "global sub-assign" \
    "int g; int main(void) { g = 10; g -= 3; return g; }" 7

run_test "narrow uchar shr-assign" \
    "int main(void) { unsigned char c = 200; c >>= 1; return c; }" 100

run_test "narrow ushort shr-assign" \
    "int main(void) { unsigned short s = 40000; s >>= 8; return s; }" 156

run_test "unsigned shr-assign loop" \
    "int main(void) { unsigned incr = 1u << 14; unsigned huff = 0x7FFF; while (huff & incr) incr >>= 1; return incr == 0; }" 1
@}

\subsection{Function Call}

A function call walks the argument list (linked via \texttt{AST\_F4}),
evaluates each argument expression, and stores the resulting vregs
into an arena-allocated array.  The array layout is
\texttt{[count, vreg0, vreg1, \ldots]} so the code generator can
marshal arguments into the ABI-correct registers or stack slots.
The \texttt{IR\_CALL} instruction stores the callee name in
\texttt{SRC1} and the argument array pointer in \texttt{SRC2}; the
destination vreg receives the return value.

When the callee has a prototype, each argument is converted to its
\emph{parameter's} type before being stored (\stdref{6.5.2.2}).  We walk a
parameter cursor (\texttt{x25}) alongside the argument list, reading the
parameter declaration's resolved type into \texttt{x26}; the function type
carries this list because a plain \texttt{TY\_FUNC} would otherwise forget
it.  An integer argument passed to a \texttt{double} parameter is what makes
this load-bearing: without the conversion the value would be marshalled in an
integer register and reinterpreted as raw bits, so \texttt{f(42)} against
\texttt{double~d} would pass garbage.  The conversion also drives register
classification---once we know the parameter is \texttt{double}, the argument
is placed in an SSE register \texttt{d0}--\texttt{d7} rather than
\texttt{x0}--\texttt{x7}.  The cursor stops once we run past the fixed
parameters (an unprototyped or variadic tail), where each argument keeps its
own promoted type.

Two GNU builtins are intercepted by name before dispatch, alongside the
variadic operators.  \texttt{\_\_builtin\_va\_start} and
\texttt{\_\_builtin\_va\_end} lower to their variadic handlers
(Section~\ref{sec:variadic-defs}), and \texttt{\_\_builtin\_expect(x, c)}
--- a branch-prediction hint --- lowers to just its first argument
\texttt{x}, since with no profile feedback the hint has no effect on the
value.  The kernel reaches this builtin constantly: \texttt{likely(e)} and
\texttt{unlikely(e)} expand to \texttt{\_\_builtin\_expect(!!(e), 1)} and
\texttt{\_\_builtin\_expect(!!(e), 0)}, wrapping arbitrary
sub-expressions, calls among them.  Were it not intercepted it would be
emitted as a direct call to an undefined external \texttt{\_\_builtin\_expect}
and fail to link.

The callee decides direct versus indirect dispatch.  A bare name is a
direct \texttt{IR\_CALL} by symbol only when it truly names a
\emph{function}: it is neither a local variable nor a global object whose
resolved type is a pointer.  Every other callee is evaluated for its
function-pointer value and dispatched through \texttt{IR\_CALL\_IND}: a
local function-pointer variable (found in the locals table), a \emph{global}
function-pointer object (a bare name typed \texttt{TY\_PTR}), or any other
expression such as a struct field \texttt{o.f}, a dereference
\texttt{(*pp)}, or a subscript \texttt{table[i]}.  The global case is easy
to miss: the name is not in the locals table, so a local-only test would
fall through to a direct call and branch into the data section (a
\texttt{SIGBUS}), when the intent is to \emph{load} the pointer from that
symbol and call through it.  A name with no resolved type---an implicit
declaration---is assumed to be a function and called directly.  Routing
every indirect callee to one path is what lets a function pointer stored in
a \texttt{struct} field, or in a file-scope variable, be called.

One subtlety on the indirect path: a \emph{dereferenced} function pointer
\texttt{(*f)(\ldots)} must not lower to a load.  In C a function
designator---which \texttt{*f} is, when \texttt{f} points to a
function---decays straight back to the function's address (\stdref{6.3.2.1}),
so \texttt{(*f)}, \texttt{(**f)}, and \texttt{f} all call the same code.  Were
we to build \texttt{(*f)} as an ordinary expression it would emit
\texttt{IR\_LOAD} of the pointer's target---reading the first eight bytes of
the callee's \emph{machine code} as if they were an address and branching
there (an \texttt{EXC\_BAD\_ACCESS}, exactly what \texttt{lua\_newstate}'s
\texttt{(*f)(ud,\ldots)} allocator call triggered).  So before evaluating the
indirect callee we peel any parentheses and any \texttt{*} whose result type
is \texttt{TY\_FUNC}, leaving the bare pointer expression to build into the
function-pointer vreg directly.

@d IR: build call @{
// -- kcc_ir_build_call ---
// uint64_t kcc_ir_build_call(node *expr)
//   x0 = AST_CALL_EXPR (F0=callee, F1=first_arg linked via F4)
// Evaluates arguments, stores arg vregs in IR_SRC2 array, emits IR_CALL.
// IR_SRC2 points to arena-allocated: [count(8), vreg0(8), vreg1(8), ...]
.text
.globl _kcc_ir_build_call
.p2align 2
_kcc_ir_build_call:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-128]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    stp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    stp x23, x24, [sp, #48]
    stp x25, x26, [sp, #112]     // x25 = param cursor, x26 = param type

    str xzr, [sp, #80]            // sret result-buffer vreg (0 = none)
    mov x19, x0                   // x19 = AST_CALL_EXPR node

    // Get callee expression
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F0]       // callee expr
    cbz x0, .Libc_done_zero
    str x0, [sp, #72]            // save callee expr node (indirect path rebuilds it)
    ldr x1, [x0, #AST_TAG]
    cmp x1, #AST_IDENT_EXPR
    b.ne .Libc_callee_indirect   // non-ident callee (o.f, (*pp), a[i]) → indirect

    ldr x20, [x0, #AST_F0]       // x20 = callee name ptr

    // Variadic builtins: va_start(ap, last) and va_end(ap) are lowered
    // specially rather than emitted as calls.  Their GNU spellings
    // __builtin_va_start / __builtin_va_end (the kernel's <stdarg.h> maps the
    // va_* macros to them) are accepted as aliases.
    mov x0, x20
    adrp x1, _kcc_ir_str_va_start@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_ir_str_va_start@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_strcmp
    cbz x0, .Libc_va_start
    mov x0, x20
    adrp x1, _kcc_ir_str_builtin_va_start@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_ir_str_builtin_va_start@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_strcmp
    cbz x0, .Libc_va_start
    mov x0, x20
    adrp x1, _kcc_ir_str_va_end@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_ir_str_va_end@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_strcmp
    cbz x0, .Libc_va_end
    mov x0, x20
    adrp x1, _kcc_ir_str_builtin_va_end@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_ir_str_builtin_va_end@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_strcmp
    cbz x0, .Libc_va_end

    // __builtin_expect(x, c) is a branch-prediction hint; with no profile
    // feedback its value is exactly its first argument, so lower it to that
    // argument and drop the hint.  The kernel's likely()/unlikely() expand to
    // it around ordinary sub-expressions (including calls), so intercepting it
    // by name here keeps those uses from becoming an undefined external.
    mov x0, x20
    adrp x1, _kcc_ir_str_builtin_expect@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_ir_str_builtin_expect@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_strcmp
    cbz x0, .Libc_builtin_expect

    // __builtin_constant_p(x): a compile-time predicate, 1 if x is an integer
    // constant expression and 0 otherwise.  The kernel gates whole code paths on
    // it; folding it to a constant here lets a constant `? :` drop its dead arm.
    mov x0, x20
    adrp x1, _kcc_ir_str_builtin_constant_p@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_ir_str_builtin_constant_p@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_strcmp
    cbz x0, .Libc_builtin_constant_p

    // Direct name, a local function-pointer variable, or a global one?
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_strlen
    mov x24, x0                   // x24 = name len
    adrp x0, _kcc_ir_locals@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_ir_locals@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x0, [x0]
    mov x1, x20
    mov x2, x24
    bl _kcc_ht_get
    // x0 = vreg+1 (with possible bit-63 tag) or 0 if not found
    cbnz x0, .Libc_disp_set      // a local variable → indirect through its vreg
    // Not a local.  A bare name whose resolved type is a pointer names a
    // global function-pointer *object*: its value lives in memory at that
    // symbol, so we must load it and dispatch indirectly, exactly as for a
    // local fn-ptr.  Emitting a direct IR_CALL to the data symbol instead
    // would branch into the data section (SIGBUS).  A name typed TY_FUNC (an
    // actual function) or with no resolved type (an implicit declaration)
    // stays a direct call by name.
    ldr x1, [sp, #72]            // callee ident node
    ldr x1, [x1, #AST_F3]        // resolved type
    cbz x1, .Libc_disp_set
    ldr x1, [x1, #TY_KIND]
    cmp x1, #TY_PTR
    b.ne .Libc_disp_set
    mov x0, #1                   // global fn-ptr object → force the indirect path
.Libc_disp_set:
    str x0, [sp, #64]            // locals_result: 0 → direct call by name
    b .Libc_count_start

.Libc_callee_indirect:
    // A callee that is any expression other than a bare name: evaluate it
    // for its function-pointer value and dispatch indirectly.
    mov x0, #1
    str x0, [sp, #64]            // force the indirect path

.Libc_count_start:
    // Count arguments (walk F4 chain)
    ldr x21, [x19, #AST_F1]      // x21 = first arg
    mov x22, #0                    // x22 = arg count
    mov x0, x21
.Libc_count_args:
    cbz x0, .Libc_count_done
    add x22, x22, #1
    ldr x0, [x0, #AST_F4]
    b .Libc_count_args
.Libc_count_done:

    // Aggregate return-by-value: if the callee returns a struct OR union,
    // allocate a result buffer whose address becomes a hidden trailing argument
    // and the call's own result value.  A union return needs this just as much
    // as a struct: without the buffer the backend still unpacks x0(-x1) into
    // DEST, storing through a never-materialised (garbage) address and faulting.
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F3]       // call result type (sema-resolved)
    cbz x0, .Libc_alloc_args
    ldr x1, [x0, #TY_KIND]
    cmp x1, #TY_STRUCT
    b.eq .Libc_ret_agg
    cmp x1, #TY_UNION
    b.ne .Libc_alloc_args
.Libc_ret_agg:
    str x0, [sp, #88]            // result aggregate type
    bl _kcc_ir_type_slots        // x0 = slot count
    str x0, [sp, #96]            // slot count
    mov x0, #IR_ALLOCA
    bl _kcc_ir_alloc_instr
    ldr x1, [sp, #88]
    str x1, [x0, #IR_TYPE]
    ldr x1, [sp, #96]
    str x1, [x0, #IR_SRC1]       // slot count
    str x0, [sp, #96]            // save instr ptr
    bl _kcc_ir_next_vreg
    ldr x1, [sp, #96]
    str x0, [x1, #IR_DEST]
    add x2, x0, #1               // bias by 1: vreg 0 is a valid buffer vreg
    str x2, [sp, #80]            // sret buffer vreg + 1 (0 = none)
    mov x0, x1
    bl _kcc_ir_emit

.Libc_alloc_args:
    // Allocate args array.  Layout: args[0] = total arg count, then two words
    // per argument: args[1+i*2] = vreg, args[2+i*2] = AAPCS64 class.  Size is
    // therefore (1 + total*2) * 8 bytes.
    ldr x0, [sp, #80]            // biased sret buffer vreg (0 = none)
    cmp x0, #0
    cset x1, ne                  // has_sret ∈ {0,1}
    add x1, x22, x1              // total argument count
    str x1, [sp, #104]          // total count (args[0])
    lsl x0, x1, #1              // total*2
    add x0, x0, #1             // 1 + total*2
    lsl x0, x0, #3               // in bytes
    bl _kcc_arena_alloc
    mov x23, x0                    // x23 = args array

    // first_stack_idx: where the caller's stack image begins.  For a variadic
    // callee it is min(nfixed, 8): with nfixed<=8 the variadic tail starts at
    // [sp,#0] (the Apple convention the system libc also uses, so interop with
    // sprintf/printf holds); with nfixed>8 it is 8, so a fixed parameter that
    // overflowed the eight argument registers lands on the stack BEFORE the
    // variadic tail rather than colliding with it.  A non-variadic call uses 1.
    ldr x1, [sp, #72]            // callee expr node
    mov x2, #1
    cbz x1, .Libc_fsi_done
    ldr x1, [x1, #AST_F3]        // callee resolved type
    cbz x1, .Libc_fsi_done
    ldr x3, [x1, #TY_KIND]
    cmp x3, #TY_PTR
    b.ne .Libc_fsi_chk
    ldr x1, [x1, #TY_F0]         // pointer-to-function → the function type
    cbz x1, .Libc_fsi_done
    ldr x3, [x1, #TY_KIND]
.Libc_fsi_chk:
    cmp x3, #TY_FUNC
    b.ne .Libc_fsi_done
    ldr x3, [x1, #TY_QUALS]      // nfixed if variadic, else 0
    cbz x3, .Libc_fsi_done
    mov x2, x3                   // fsi = nfixed
    cmp x2, #8
    b.ls .Libc_fsi_done
    mov x2, #8                   // clamp: nfixed>8 → fsi = 8
.Libc_fsi_done:
    ldr x0, [sp, #104]           // total count
    orr x0, x0, x2, lsl #32
    str x0, [x23]                // args[0] = count | (first_stack_idx << 32)

    // Parameter cursor for prototyped conversion/classification.  The callee's
    // resolved type carries the param declaration list in TY_F1 (0 when
    // unprototyped or once variadic overflow begins); a function-pointer callee
    // is dereferenced one level to reach the function type.
    mov x25, #0
    ldr x0, [sp, #72]            // callee expr node
    cbz x0, .Libc_params_ready
    ldr x0, [x0, #AST_F3]        // callee resolved type
    cbz x0, .Libc_params_ready
    ldr x1, [x0, #TY_KIND]
    cmp x1, #TY_FUNC
    b.eq .Libc_params_func
    cmp x1, #TY_PTR
    b.ne .Libc_params_ready
    ldr x0, [x0, #TY_F0]        // pointee
    cbz x0, .Libc_params_ready
    ldr x1, [x0, #TY_KIND]
    cmp x1, #TY_FUNC
    b.ne .Libc_params_ready
.Libc_params_func:
    ldr x25, [x0, #TY_F1]       // param list
.Libc_params_ready:

    // Evaluate each argument expression: store its vreg and AAPCS64 class.
    mov x24, x21                   // x24 = current arg node
    mov x21, #0                    // x21 = arg index
.Libc_eval_args:
    cmp x21, x22
    b.ge .Libc_eval_done
    // Resolve this position's parameter type (x26 = 0 for variadic overflow or
    // an unprototyped callee), advancing the param cursor.
    mov x26, #0
    cbz x25, .Libc_ptype_done
    ldr x0, [x25, #AST_F0]      // param type node
    cbz x0, .Libc_ptype_adv
    bl _kcc_sema_resolve_type
    mov x26, x0                 // x26 = parameter type
.Libc_ptype_adv:
    ldr x25, [x25, #AST_F4]     // advance param cursor
.Libc_ptype_done:
    // Classify by the parameter type when known (so an int passed to a double
    // parameter lands in an SSE register), else by the argument's own type.
    cbnz x26, .Libc_classify_param
    ldr x0, [x24, #AST_F3]      // argument's resolved type
    b .Libc_classify_go
.Libc_classify_param:
    mov x0, x26
.Libc_classify_go:
    bl _kcc_ir_classify_type
    str x0, [sp, #104]           // stash class
    cmp x0, #2
    b.lt .Libc_eval_scalar
    mov x0, x24
    bl _kcc_ir_build_struct_addr  // vreg holding the struct's address
    b .Libc_eval_store
.Libc_eval_scalar:
    mov x0, x24
    bl _kcc_ir_build_expr          // returns vreg in x0
    // Convert the argument to the parameter type (int<->double) when prototyped.
    cbz x26, .Libc_eval_store
    ldr x1, [x24, #AST_F3]      // from = argument type
    mov x2, x26                 // to = parameter type
    bl _kcc_ir_convert
.Libc_eval_store:
    lsl x1, x21, #1              // index*2
    add x1, x1, #1             // 1 + index*2
    str x0, [x23, x1, lsl #3]     // vreg
    add x1, x1, #1
    ldr x2, [sp, #104]           // class
    str x2, [x23, x1, lsl #3]     // class
    ldr x24, [x24, #AST_F4]       // next arg
    add x21, x21, #1
    b .Libc_eval_args
.Libc_eval_done:

    // Append the hidden result pointer with class X8 (5): the marshal loads
    // it into x8 (AAPCS64 indirect-result register).  A >16B callee reads it;
    // a <=16B callee ignores x8 and returns the aggregate in x0(-x1) instead.
    ldr x0, [sp, #80]            // biased sret buffer vreg
    cbz x0, .Libc_dispatch
    sub x0, x0, #1              // recover real buffer vreg
    lsl x1, x22, #1             // arg_count*2
    add x1, x1, #1             // 1 + arg_count*2
    str x0, [x23, x1, lsl #3]    // vreg = buffer address
    add x1, x1, #1
    mov x2, #5
    str x2, [x23, x1, lsl #3]   // class = X8

.Libc_dispatch:
    // Check if indirect call (callee is a local variable)
    ldr x0, [sp, #64]            // locals_result
    cbnz x0, .Libc_indirect

    // Direct call: emit IR_CALL.  IR_TYPE = the call's result type, so the
    // backend knows whether the result comes back in x0 or d0.
    mov x0, #IR_CALL
    bl _kcc_ir_alloc_instr
    str x20, [x0, #IR_SRC1]       // callee name
    str x23, [x0, #IR_SRC2]       // args array ptr
    ldr x1, [x19, #AST_F3]       // call result type (x19 = call node)
    cbnz x1, .Libc_dtype
    adrp x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x1, [x1]
.Libc_dtype:
    str x1, [x0, #IR_TYPE]
    mov x19, x0
    // DEST is the sret buffer vreg for a struct return (so the backend can
    // unpack x0/x1 into it), otherwise a fresh result vreg.
    ldr x1, [sp, #80]
    cbz x1, .Libc_dfresh
    sub x1, x1, #1
    str x1, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    b .Libc_demit
.Libc_dfresh:
    bl _kcc_ir_next_vreg
    str x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
.Libc_demit:
    mov x0, x19
    bl _kcc_ir_emit
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]        // result vreg (.Libc_done overrides for struct)
    b .Libc_done

.Libc_indirect:
    // Indirect call: build callee expression to get function pointer vreg.
    // First peel parentheses and any dereference of a function pointer:
    // `(*f)` is a function designator identical to `f`, so building it as a
    // plain expression would wrongly LOAD through the pointer.
    ldr x1, [sp, #72]            // callee expr node
.Libc_ind_unwrap:
    ldr x2, [x1, #AST_TAG]
    cmp x2, #AST_PAREN_EXPR
    b.ne .Libc_ind_chkderef
    ldr x3, [x1, #AST_F0]        // inner expression
    cbz x3, .Libc_ind_build
    mov x1, x3
    b .Libc_ind_unwrap
.Libc_ind_chkderef:
    cmp x2, #AST_UNARY_EXPR
    b.ne .Libc_ind_build
    ldr x3, [x1, #AST_F0]        // unary op
    cmp x3, #TOK_STAR
    b.ne .Libc_ind_build
    ldr x3, [x1, #AST_F3]        // deref result type
    cbz x3, .Libc_ind_build
    ldr x3, [x3, #TY_KIND]
    cmp x3, #TY_FUNC             // *f where f -> function: designator, no load
    b.ne .Libc_ind_build
    ldr x3, [x1, #AST_F1]        // operand (the function pointer expression)
    cbz x3, .Libc_ind_build
    mov x1, x3
    b .Libc_ind_unwrap
.Libc_ind_build:
    mov x0, x1
    bl _kcc_ir_build_expr          // returns fn-ptr vreg in x0
    mov x24, x0                    // x24 = fn-ptr vreg

    // Emit IR_CALL_IND (result type in IR_TYPE, as for the direct call)
    mov x0, #IR_CALL_IND
    bl _kcc_ir_alloc_instr
    str x24, [x0, #IR_SRC1]       // fn-ptr vreg
    str x23, [x0, #IR_SRC2]       // args array ptr
    ldr x1, [x19, #AST_F3]       // call result type (x19 = call node)
    cbnz x1, .Libc_itype
    adrp x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x1, [x1]
.Libc_itype:
    str x1, [x0, #IR_TYPE]
    mov x19, x0
    ldr x1, [sp, #80]
    cbz x1, .Libc_ifresh
    sub x1, x1, #1
    str x1, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    b .Libc_iemit
.Libc_ifresh:
    bl _kcc_ir_next_vreg
    str x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
.Libc_iemit:
    mov x0, x19
    bl _kcc_ir_emit
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    b .Libc_done

.Libc_va_start:
    mov x0, x19                 // call node
    bl _kcc_ir_build_va_start
    b .Libc_epilogue

.Libc_va_end:
    mov x0, #0                  // va_end is a no-op in this ABI
    b .Libc_epilogue

.Libc_builtin_expect:
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F1]      // first argument (the predicted expression)
    bl _kcc_ir_build_expr       // its vreg is the whole builtin's value
    b .Libc_epilogue

.Libc_builtin_constant_p:
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F1]      // first argument
    bl _kcc_expr_is_ice         // 1 if a constant expression, else 0
    bl _kcc_ir_emit_const       // materialise that 0/1
    b .Libc_epilogue

.Libc_done_zero:
    mov x0, #0
.Libc_done:
    // A struct-returning call yields the address of its result buffer,
    // not the (unused) scalar result vreg.
    ldr x1, [sp, #80]           // biased sret buffer vreg
    cbz x1, .Libc_epilogue
    sub x0, x1, #1              // recover real buffer vreg
.Libc_epilogue:
    ldp x25, x26, [sp, #112]
    ldp x23, x24, [sp, #48]
    ldp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #128
    ret
@| _kcc_ir_build_call
@}

\subsection{Variadic Function Definitions}
\label{sec:variadic-defs}

A function \emph{defined} with a trailing \texttt{...} reads its unnamed
arguments through the \texttt{<stdarg.h>} interface---\texttt{va\_list},
\texttt{va\_start}, \texttt{va\_arg}, \texttt{va\_end}.  \kcc{} provides
these as built-ins rather than a header.  The call lowering pushes the
variadic tail onto the stack (the Apple variadic ABI push), so a variadic
callee needs only a cursor into that stack area.  Accordingly
\texttt{va\_list} is an opaque eight-byte pointer (resolved by sema);
\texttt{va\_start(ap, last)} points the cursor at the first variadic
argument; \texttt{va\_arg(ap, T)} reads the eight-byte slot at the cursor
and advances it; and \texttt{va\_end} is a no-op.

\kcc{} also answers to the GNU \texttt{\_\_builtin\_} spellings of these
operators---\texttt{\_\_builtin\_va\_start}, \texttt{\_\_builtin\_va\_arg},
\texttt{\_\_builtin\_va\_end}, and the type \texttt{\_\_builtin\_va\_list}---as
exact aliases for the short names.  A freestanding \texttt{<stdarg.h>} (the
kernel's, reached under \texttt{-nostdinc}) is written entirely in terms of
these builtins: \texttt{typedef \_\_builtin\_va\_list va\_list;} and
\texttt{\#define va\_arg(v,\,T) \_\_builtin\_va\_arg(v,\,T)}, so once the macros
expand it is the \texttt{\_\_builtin\_} form that reaches the parser and the IR
builder.  Recognising both spellings lets \kcc{} consume such a header without a
built-in \texttt{<stdarg.h>} of its own.  (\texttt{va\_copy}/%
\texttt{\_\_builtin\_va\_copy} is not yet provided.)

Both sides depend on the prototype's fixed-parameter count.  Sema records it
when it checks a function declaration, storing the count in the otherwise
unused \texttt{TY\_QUALS} slot of the \texttt{TY\_FUNC} descriptor (zero there
means non-variadic); \texttt{kcc\_ir\_build\_call} reads it back from the
callee's type to choose the stack layout, and the caller places the variadic
tail at \texttt{[sp,\,\#0]} of the pushed argument area
(\texttt{kcc\_cg\_marshal\_args} indexes the stack image from that count), so
in the callee the first variadic argument
lives at \texttt{[x29 + frame\_size]}---independent of how many fixed
parameters preceded it, and with \texttt{frame\_size} known only in the
backend.  \texttt{va\_start} therefore lowers to a dedicated
\texttt{IR\_VA\_START} instruction; the code generator materialises that
address and \texttt{va\_start} stores it into \texttt{ap}.  (The instruction
still records the fixed count $F$ in \texttt{SRC2} for diagnostics, though the
offset no longer depends on it.)  \texttt{va\_arg}
needs no new opcode: it loads the cursor, dereferences it, adds eight, and
writes the cursor back, all with ordinary IR.  Both builtins locate
\texttt{ap} through \texttt{\_kcc\_ir\_ident\_alloca}, which resolves a
local identifier to its alloca vreg.

@d IR: variadic builtins @{
// -- kcc_ir_ident_alloca ---
// uint64_t kcc_ir_ident_alloca(node *ident)
//   x0 = AST_IDENT_EXPR.  Returns the identifier's alloca vreg, or ~0 when
//   it is not a known local.
.text
.globl _kcc_ir_ident_alloca
.p2align 2
_kcc_ir_ident_alloca:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    cbz x0, .Lia_err
    // The va_list expression is usually a plain identifier, but the standard
    // allows any lvalue --- an array element (`aps[4]`) or a struct member
    // (`a.g`).  Route those to the subscript/member address builders, whose
    // result is exactly the va_list storage address the callers want.
    ldr x1, [x0, #AST_TAG]
    cmp x1, #AST_IDENT_EXPR
    b.eq .Lia_ident
    cmp x1, #AST_SUBSCRIPT
    b.eq .Lia_subscript_lv
    cmp x1, #AST_MEMBER
    b.eq .Lia_member_lv
    b .Lia_err
.Lia_subscript_lv:
    bl _kcc_ir_build_subscript_addr
    b .Lia_done
.Lia_member_lv:
    bl _kcc_ir_build_member_addr
    b .Lia_done
.Lia_ident:
    ldr x19, [x0, #AST_F0]       // name ptr
    cbz x19, .Lia_err
    mov x0, x19
    bl _kcc_strlen
    mov x2, x0
    adrp x0, _kcc_ir_locals@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_ir_locals@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x0, [x0]
    mov x1, x19
    bl _kcc_ht_get
    cbz x0, .Lia_none
    bic x0, x0, #0x8000000000000000
    sub x0, x0, #1
    b .Lia_done
.Lia_err:
    mov x0, #-1
    b .Lia_done
.Lia_none:
    // Not a local: a file-scope `va_list` (e.g. a global `va_list gap`).  Its
    // storage address is a global reference, which serves exactly as the local
    // alloca vreg would --- va_start/va_arg both want the address of the
    // va_list object.  x19 still holds the interned name.
    mov x0, #IR_GLOBAL_REF
    bl _kcc_ir_alloc_instr
    str x19, [x0, #IR_SRC1]      // symbol name
    adrp x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x1, [x1]
    str x1, [x0, #IR_TYPE]
    mov x20, x0
    bl _kcc_ir_next_vreg
    str x0, [x20, #IR_DEST]
    mov x19, x0                  // global-ref vreg
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_ir_emit
    mov x0, x19
.Lia_done:
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #32
    ret

// -- kcc_ir_build_va_start ---
// void kcc_ir_build_va_start(node *call)   — call = va_start(ap, last)
.text
.globl _kcc_ir_build_va_start
.p2align 2
_kcc_ir_build_va_start:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-48]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    stp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    mov x19, x0                  // call node
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F1]       // first arg = ap ident
    bl _kcc_ir_ident_alloca
    mov x20, x0                  // ap alloca vreg
    // F = current function's fixed-parameter count
    adrp x0, _kcc_ir_cur_func@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_ir_cur_func@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x0, [x0]
    ldr x21, [x0, #IRF_PARAMS]
    // IR_VA_START: DEST = base-address vreg, SRC2 = F
    mov x0, #IR_VA_START
    bl _kcc_ir_alloc_instr
    mov x22, x0
    adrp x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x1, [x1]
    str x1, [x22, #IR_TYPE]
    str x21, [x22, #IR_SRC2]
    bl _kcc_ir_next_vreg
    str x0, [x22, #IR_DEST]
    mov x21, x0                  // base-address vreg
    mov x0, x22
    bl _kcc_ir_emit
    // *ap = base
    mov x0, #IR_STORE
    bl _kcc_ir_alloc_instr
    mov x22, x0
    adrp x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x1, [x1]
    str x1, [x22, #IR_TYPE]
    str x21, [x22, #IR_SRC1]     // value = base address
    str x20, [x22, #IR_SRC2]     // pointer = ap's storage
    mov x0, x22
    bl _kcc_ir_emit
    mov x0, x21
    ldp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #48
    ret

// -- kcc_ir_build_va_arg ---
// uint64_t kcc_ir_build_va_arg(node *va_arg)
//   Reads one argument from the va_list cursor and advances it by one 8-byte
//   slot.  For a scalar the loaded 8 bytes are the value.  A struct/union is
//   passed through `...` by \emph{address} in kcc's ABI (the caller's
//   marshal-args stores the object's address in the variadic stack slot, see
//   \S\ref{sec:aapcs64-marshal}), so the same loaded 8 bytes ARE the object's
//   address --- exactly what an aggregate rvalue is elsewhere --- and the
//   caller (a struct copy) reads the object through it.
.text
.globl _kcc_ir_build_va_arg
.p2align 2
_kcc_ir_build_va_arg:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-64]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    stp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    stp x23, x24, [sp, #48]
    mov x19, x0                  // va_arg node
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F0]       // ap ident
    bl _kcc_ir_ident_alloca
    mov x20, x0                  // ap alloca vreg
    // p = *ap  (current cursor)
    mov x0, #IR_LOAD
    bl _kcc_ir_alloc_instr
    mov x22, x0
    adrp x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x1, [x1]
    str x1, [x22, #IR_TYPE]
    str x20, [x22, #IR_SRC1]
    bl _kcc_ir_next_vreg
    str x0, [x22, #IR_DEST]
    mov x21, x0                  // p
    mov x0, x22
    bl _kcc_ir_emit
    // val = *p  (one 8-byte argument slot: a scalar value, or a struct address)
    mov x0, #IR_LOAD
    bl _kcc_ir_alloc_instr
    mov x22, x0
    adrp x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x1, [x1]
    str x1, [x22, #IR_TYPE]
    str x21, [x22, #IR_SRC1]
    bl _kcc_ir_next_vreg
    str x0, [x22, #IR_DEST]
    mov x23, x0                  // val
    mov x0, x22
    bl _kcc_ir_emit
    // c8 = 8
    mov x0, #IR_CONST
    bl _kcc_ir_alloc_instr
    mov x22, x0
    adrp x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x1, [x1]
    str x1, [x22, #IR_TYPE]
    mov x1, #8
    str x1, [x22, #IR_SRC1]
    bl _kcc_ir_next_vreg
    str x0, [x22, #IR_DEST]
    mov x24, x0                  // c8
    mov x0, x22
    bl _kcc_ir_emit
    // p2 = p + 8
    mov x0, #IR_ADD
    bl _kcc_ir_alloc_instr
    mov x22, x0
    adrp x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x1, [x1]
    str x1, [x22, #IR_TYPE]
    str x21, [x22, #IR_SRC1]
    str x24, [x22, #IR_SRC2]
    bl _kcc_ir_next_vreg
    str x0, [x22, #IR_DEST]
    mov x21, x0                  // p2
    mov x0, x22
    bl _kcc_ir_emit
    // *ap = p2
    mov x0, #IR_STORE
    bl _kcc_ir_alloc_instr
    mov x22, x0
    adrp x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x1, [x1]
    str x1, [x22, #IR_TYPE]
    str x21, [x22, #IR_SRC1]
    str x20, [x22, #IR_SRC2]
    mov x0, x22
    bl _kcc_ir_emit
    mov x0, x23                  // return val (scalar value, or struct address)
    ldp x23, x24, [sp, #48]
    ldp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #64
    ret

.section __TEXT,__cstring,cstring_literals
_kcc_ir_str_va_start:    .asciz "va_start"
_kcc_ir_str_va_end:      .asciz "va_end"
_kcc_ir_str_builtin_va_start: .asciz "__builtin_va_start"
_kcc_ir_str_builtin_va_end:   .asciz "__builtin_va_end"
_kcc_ir_str_builtin_expect:   .asciz "__builtin_expect"
_kcc_ir_str_builtin_constant_p: .asciz "__builtin_constant_p"
.text
@| _kcc_ir_ident_alloca _kcc_ir_build_va_start _kcc_ir_build_va_arg
@| _kcc_ir_str_va_start _kcc_ir_str_va_end
@| _kcc_ir_str_builtin_va_start _kcc_ir_str_builtin_va_end
@| _kcc_ir_str_builtin_expect _kcc_ir_str_builtin_constant_p
@}

\paragraph{End-to-end tests.}
The \texttt{IR\_CALL} instruction carries a callee name and an
arena-allocated argument array.  The code generator loads each
argument vreg from its stack slot into \texttt{x0}--\texttt{x7}
(following AAPCS64), emits a \texttt{BL} with an
\texttt{ARM64\_RELOC\_BRANCH26} relocation, and stores the return
value from \texttt{x0} into the destination vreg's slot.  Locally
defined functions become \texttt{N\_SECT|N\_EXT} symbols; external
calls become \texttt{N\_UNDF|N\_EXT} symbols resolved by the linker.
The recursive factorial test is particularly demanding: it verifies
that the prologue and epilogue correctly save and restore the frame
pointer and link register across a deep call chain.

@d Test: codegen functions @{
echo ""
echo "--- Functions ---"

run_test "simple call" \
    "int foo(int x) { return x + 1; } int main(void) { return foo(41); }" 42

run_test "nested calls" \
    "int foo(int x) { return x + 1; } int main(void) { return foo(foo(1)); }" 3

run_test "multi-arg" \
    "int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; } int main(void) { return add(20, 22); }" 42

run_test "three args" \
    "int sum3(int a, int b, int c) { return a + b + c; } int main(void) { return sum3(10, 20, 12); }" 42

run_test "recursive factorial" \
    "int fact(int n) { if (n <= 1) return 1; return n * fact(n - 1); } int main(void) { return fact(5); }" 120
@}

\subsection{String Literal}

A string literal is represented by \texttt{IR\_STRING\_LIT}, which
carries the data pointer in \texttt{SRC1} and the byte length in
\texttt{SRC2}.  The code generator
(Chapter~\ref{ch:codegen}) later appends this data to the
\texttt{\_\_text} section and emits a PC-relative reference so the
vreg holds the string's run-time address.

@d IR: build string literal @{
// -- kcc_ir_build_string_lit ---
// uint64_t kcc_ir_build_string_lit(node *lit)
//   x0 = AST_STR_LIT (F0=str_ptr, F1=str_len)
// Returns vreg holding the string's address.
.text
.globl _kcc_ir_build_string_lit
.p2align 2
_kcc_ir_build_string_lit:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-48]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    str x21, [sp, #32]

    mov x19, x0
    ldr x20, [x19, #AST_F0]     // str_ptr
    ldr x21, [x19, #AST_F1]     // str_len

    mov x0, #IR_STRING_LIT
    bl _kcc_ir_alloc_instr
    mov x19, x0                  // save instr

    str x20, [x19, #IR_SRC1]    // str data pointer
    str x21, [x19, #IR_SRC2]    // str length

    // Type: use int for now (value is a 64-bit pointer)
    adrp x0, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x0, [x0]
    str x0, [x19, #IR_TYPE]

    bl _kcc_ir_next_vreg
    str x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    mov x20, x0                  // save vreg

    mov x0, x19
    bl _kcc_ir_emit

    mov x0, x20                  // return vreg

    ldr x21, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #48
    ret
@| _kcc_ir_build_string_lit
@}

\subsection{Conditional Expression}

The ternary operator \texttt{cond ? then : else} is lowered to three
fresh basic blocks plus a temporary alloca that serves as a rendezvous
point.  The condition vreg feeds an \texttt{IR\_BR\_COND} that routes
control to the \emph{then\_bb} or \emph{else\_bb}.  Each arm evaluates
its expression and \emph{stores} the result into the shared alloca,
then branches unconditionally to the \emph{merge\_bb}.  The merge block
loads the result back from the alloca, producing a single vreg that
the caller can reference regardless of which branch executed.

This store/load pattern replaces the earlier approach of ``returning
the last vreg,'' which failed because the then-branch vreg was never
referenced in the merge block and DCE eliminated it.  The alloca
approach works naturally with the all-spill code generator and avoids
phi nodes entirely.

@d IR: build conditional @{
// -- kcc_ir_build_cond_expr ---
// uint64_t kcc_ir_build_cond_expr(node *expr)
//   x0 = AST_COND_EXPR (F0=cond, F1=then, F2=else)
// Lowers to: alloca tmp → br_cond → then: store → br merge
//                                  → else: store → br merge
//            merge: load tmp → return vreg
.text
.globl _kcc_ir_build_cond_expr
.p2align 2
_kcc_ir_build_cond_expr:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-80]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    stp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    stp x23, x24, [sp, #48]
    str x25, [sp, #64]

    mov x19, x0

    // Allocate a temporary slot for the result
    mov x0, #IR_ALLOCA
    bl _kcc_ir_alloc_instr
    adrp x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x1, [x1]
    str x1, [x0, #IR_TYPE]
    mov x20, x0                  // x20 = alloca instr
    bl _kcc_ir_next_vreg
    str x0, [x20, #IR_DEST]
    mov x25, x0                  // x25 = alloca vreg (result slot)
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_ir_emit

    // Build condition
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F0]
    bl _kcc_ir_build_expr
    mov x20, x0                  // cond vreg

    // Create then/else/merge BBs
    bl _kcc_ir_alloc_bb
    mov x21, x0                  // then_bb
    bl _kcc_ir_alloc_bb
    mov x22, x0                  // else_bb
    bl _kcc_ir_alloc_bb
    mov x23, x0                  // merge_bb

    // Branch on condition
    mov x0, #IR_BR_COND
    bl _kcc_ir_alloc_instr
    str x20, [x0, #IR_DEST]
    ldr x1, [x21, #BB_ID]
    str x1, [x0, #IR_SRC1]
    ldr x1, [x22, #BB_ID]
    str x1, [x0, #IR_SRC2]
    bl _kcc_ir_emit
    adrp x0, _kcc_ir_cur_bb@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_ir_cur_bb@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x0, [x0]
    ldr x1, [x0, #BB_FLAGS]
    orr x1, x1, #BB_FLAG_TERMINATED
    str x1, [x0, #BB_FLAGS]

    // Then branch: evaluate, store to alloca, branch to merge
    mov x0, x21
    bl _kcc_ir_set_bb
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F1]
    cbz x0, .Lcond_elvis_then    // GNU `a ?: b': reuse the condition value
    bl _kcc_ir_build_expr
    mov x24, x0                  // then_vreg
    b .Lcond_then_stored
.Lcond_elvis_then:
    mov x24, x20                 // omitted middle: the condition (x20) is the value
.Lcond_then_stored:

    mov x0, #IR_STORE
    bl _kcc_ir_alloc_instr
    adrp x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x1, [x1]
    str x1, [x0, #IR_TYPE]
    str x24, [x0, #IR_SRC1]     // value
    str x25, [x0, #IR_SRC2]     // alloca
    bl _kcc_ir_emit

    mov x0, #IR_BR
    bl _kcc_ir_alloc_instr
    ldr x1, [x23, #BB_ID]
    str x1, [x0, #IR_SRC1]
    bl _kcc_ir_emit
    adrp x0, _kcc_ir_cur_bb@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_ir_cur_bb@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x0, [x0]
    ldr x1, [x0, #BB_FLAGS]
    orr x1, x1, #BB_FLAG_TERMINATED
    str x1, [x0, #BB_FLAGS]

    // Else branch: evaluate, store to alloca, branch to merge
    mov x0, x22
    bl _kcc_ir_set_bb
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F2]
    bl _kcc_ir_build_expr
    mov x24, x0                  // else_vreg

    mov x0, #IR_STORE
    bl _kcc_ir_alloc_instr
    adrp x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x1, [x1]
    str x1, [x0, #IR_TYPE]
    str x24, [x0, #IR_SRC1]     // value
    str x25, [x0, #IR_SRC2]     // alloca
    bl _kcc_ir_emit

    mov x0, #IR_BR
    bl _kcc_ir_alloc_instr
    ldr x1, [x23, #BB_ID]
    str x1, [x0, #IR_SRC1]
    bl _kcc_ir_emit
    adrp x0, _kcc_ir_cur_bb@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_ir_cur_bb@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x0, [x0]
    ldr x1, [x0, #BB_FLAGS]
    orr x1, x1, #BB_FLAG_TERMINATED
    str x1, [x0, #BB_FLAGS]

    // Merge: load from alloca → result vreg
    mov x0, x23
    bl _kcc_ir_set_bb

    mov x0, #IR_LOAD
    bl _kcc_ir_alloc_instr
    adrp x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_type_int_s@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x1, [x1]
    str x1, [x0, #IR_TYPE]
    str x25, [x0, #IR_SRC1]     // alloca vreg
    mov x20, x0
    bl _kcc_ir_next_vreg
    str x0, [x20, #IR_DEST]
    mov x24, x0                  // result vreg
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_ir_emit

    mov x0, x24                  // return result vreg

    ldr x25, [sp, #64]
    ldp x23, x24, [sp, #48]
    ldp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #80
    ret
@| _kcc_ir_build_cond_expr
@}

\paragraph{End-to-end tests.}
The alloca rendezvous pattern ensures that whichever branch executes,
the result is stored to a known location and loaded in the merge block.
These tests cover both truth values, variable conditions, initialiser
context, nesting, use as a function argument, and sub-expression
evaluation within the arms.

@d Test: codegen ternary @{
echo ""
echo "--- Ternary Operator ---"

run_test "ternary-true" \
    "int main(void) { return 1 ? 42 : 99; }" 42

run_test "ternary-false" \
    "int main(void) { return 0 ? 42 : 99; }" 99

run_test "ternary-var-cond" \
    "int main(void) { int x = 1; return x ? 10 : 20; }" 10

run_test "ternary-var-init" \
    "int main(void) { int x = 1 ? 5 : 9; return x; }" 5

run_test "ternary-nested" \
    "int main(void) { return 1 ? (0 ? 1 : 2) : 3; }" 2

run_test "ternary-expr-arms" \
    "int main(void) { int a=10; int b=3; return 1 ? a+b : a-b; }" 13

run_test "ternary-false-path" \
    "int main(void) { int a=10; int b=3; return 0 ? a+b : a-b; }" 7
@}

\section{Building Cast Expressions}

An explicit cast \texttt{(T)e} compares the target type~\texttt{T}
with the source type of the inner expression.  Any cast that touches a
floating type is delegated to \texttt{\_kcc\_ir\_convert}; the two
\texttt{\_kcc\_ir\_type\_is\_fp} probes are branched on \emph{immediately}
rather than OR-ed together, because \texttt{is\_fp} uses \texttt{x1} as
scratch and a stale target result would poison the decision --- an early
version held \texttt{target\_is\_fp} in \texttt{x1} across the second call,
so every integer cast saw a non-zero type kind there and was misrouted into
the FP path (which no-ops integer resizes), silently dropping the conversion.

For integer targets, when the target is strictly narrower (by
\texttt{TY\_SIZE}) the builder emits \texttt{IR\_TRUNC} to mask the result to
the target width; when it is wider, it emits \texttt{IR\_SEXT} for signed
source types and \texttt{IR\_ZEXT} for unsigned ones, with the source width in
\texttt{IR\_SRC2}.  Equal-width casts are \emph{not} always no-ops: a sub-word
value (\texttt{char}/\texttt{short}) is physically held in a register extended
per its own signedness, so a same-size cast that only flips signedness must
re-extend the low \texttt{size} bytes to the target's convention
(\texttt{IR\_ZEXT} towards unsigned, \texttt{IR\_SEXT} towards signed).
Without this, \texttt{(unsigned char)(signed char)0xFF} stays $-1$ instead of
$255$ --- precisely the misread that made Lua's \texttt{zgetc} treat every
\texttt{0xFF} byte as \texttt{EOZ} and reject valid bytecode as a truncated
chunk.  Word-width targets ($\geq 4$ bytes) share a bit pattern under
AArch64's zero-extending 32-bit operations and are left unchanged.

@d IR: build cast @{
// -- kcc_ir_build_cast ---
// uint64_t kcc_ir_build_cast(node *cast)
//   x0 = AST_CAST_EXPR (F0=type, F1=expr, F3=resolved target type)
// Returns vreg.
.text
.globl _kcc_ir_build_cast
.p2align 2
_kcc_ir_build_cast:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-64]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    stp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    str x23, [sp, #48]

    mov x19, x0                  // cast node

    // Save inner expr pointer before building
    ldr x20, [x19, #AST_F1]     // inner expression node

    // Build inner expression
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_ir_build_expr
    mov x21, x0                  // src_vreg

    // Get target type from cast node's F3 (set by sema)
    ldr x22, [x19, #AST_F3]
    cbz x22, .Lbc_done

    // Get source type from inner expr's F3
    ldr x23, [x20, #AST_F3]
    cbz x23, .Lbc_done

    // Any conversion touching a floating type is delegated to
    // _kcc_ir_convert, which inserts sitofp / fptosi as needed and rounds to
    // single precision when the target is float.  Integer-only casts fall
    // through to the width-resize path below.  We test each operand
    // separately and branch immediately: holding one is_fp result across the
    // second call is unsafe, because _kcc_ir_type_is_fp clobbers x1 as scratch
    // (it reads TY_KIND into x1).  A stale x1 OR-ed into the decision would be
    // a non-zero type kind, mis-routing every integer cast into _kcc_ir_convert
    // (which no-ops integer width changes), silently dropping the conversion.
    mov x0, x22
    bl _kcc_ir_type_is_fp
    cbnz x0, .Lbc_fp_convert
    mov x0, x23
    bl _kcc_ir_type_is_fp
    cbz x0, .Lbc_int_resize
.Lbc_fp_convert:
    mov x0, x21                  // src vreg
    mov x1, x23                  // from type
    mov x2, x22                  // to type
    bl _kcc_ir_convert
    mov x21, x0
    b .Lbc_done

.Lbc_int_resize:
    // A cast to _Bool normalises to 0/1, not a low-byte truncation.
    ldr x0, [x22, #TY_KIND]
    cmp x0, #TY_BOOL
    b.ne .Lbc_resize_sizes
    mov x0, x21
    bl _kcc_ir_emit_bool_norm
    mov x21, x0
    b .Lbc_done
.Lbc_resize_sizes:
    // Compare sizes
    ldr x1, [x22, #TY_SIZE]     // target size
    ldr x2, [x23, #TY_SIZE]     // source size

    cmp x1, x2
    b.eq .Lbc_same_size          // same width: maybe re-extend for signedness
    // Both narrowing and widening re-canonicalise to the target: extend the low
    // target-width bytes by the target's signedness (SEXT/ZEXT below).  A single
    // SEXT/ZEXT with source-width = target width does the narrow (it ignores the
    // upper source bytes) and the widen (the low bytes are already the value)
    // alike, and always leaves the register in the target type's canonical form.
    b .Lbc_extend

.Lbc_same_size:
    // Equal byte width.  A value narrower than 8 bytes is physically held in a
    // 64-bit register sign- or zero-extended according to its own type, so a
    // cast that only flips signedness must re-extend the low `size` bytes to
    // match the TARGET type.  Two cases motivate this.  At sub-word width,
    // (unsigned char)(signed char)0xFF must become 255, not -1 -- exactly how
    // Lua's zgetc misread every 0xFF byte as EOZ.  At word width (4 bytes) the
    // same applies whenever the source's upper 32 bits are dirty: a negative
    // constant like -1 is held as 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF, so (unsigned)-1 must be
    // masked to 0xFFFFFFFF -- otherwise `((unsigned)-1 >> 2) + 1` folds to 0
    // instead of 0x40000000 (zlib's gz_comp used exactly that for its write
    // chunk size, and a 0 made it loop forever writing nothing).  Only a full
    // 8-byte value uses every bit already and needs no re-extension.
    cmp x1, #8
    b.hs .Lbc_done
    ldr x3, [x22, #TY_F0]        // target signedness (1=signed, 0=unsigned)
    ldr x4, [x23, #TY_F0]        // source signedness
    cmp x3, x4
    b.eq .Lbc_done               // same signedness: nothing to materialise
    cbz x3, .Lbc_ss_zext
    mov x0, #IR_SEXT
    b .Lbc_ss_emit
.Lbc_ss_zext:
    mov x0, #IR_ZEXT
.Lbc_ss_emit:
    bl _kcc_ir_alloc_instr
    mov x20, x0
    str x22, [x20, #IR_TYPE]    // target type
    str x21, [x20, #IR_SRC1]    // source vreg
    ldr x0, [x22, #TY_SIZE]     // re-extend from `size` bytes
    str x0, [x20, #IR_SRC2]
    bl _kcc_ir_next_vreg
    str x0, [x20, #IR_DEST]
    mov x21, x0
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_ir_emit
    b .Lbc_done

.Lbc_trunc:
    mov x0, #IR_TRUNC
    bl _kcc_ir_alloc_instr
    mov x20, x0

    str x22, [x20, #IR_TYPE]    // target type
    str x21, [x20, #IR_SRC1]    // source vreg

    bl _kcc_ir_next_vreg
    str x0, [x20, #IR_DEST]
    mov x21, x0

    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_ir_emit
    b .Lbc_done

.Lbc_extend:
    // Re-canonicalise to the TARGET's representation: extend the value per the
    // TARGET's signedness and width.  The result's low target-width bits are
    // already correct in the source register, so extending from the target
    // width by the target's sign gives the right 64-bit value.  Using the
    // SOURCE's signedness here sign-extended a signed-to-wider-unsigned cast
    // like (unsigned short)(signed char)-1 to 0xFFFF...FF instead of 65535, so
    // a following bitwise operation saw dirty upper bits and computed the wrong
    // result (a char/short xor promoted to int).
    ldr x0, [x22, #TY_F0]       // TARGET signedness (1=signed, 0=unsigned)
    cbz x0, .Lbc_zext
    mov x0, #IR_SEXT
    b .Lbc_ext_emit
.Lbc_zext:
    mov x0, #IR_ZEXT

.Lbc_ext_emit:
    bl _kcc_ir_alloc_instr
    mov x20, x0

    str x22, [x20, #IR_TYPE]    // target type
    str x21, [x20, #IR_SRC1]    // source vreg
    ldr x0, [x22, #TY_SIZE]     // TARGET width in bytes
    str x0, [x20, #IR_SRC2]     // pass to codegen

    bl _kcc_ir_next_vreg
    str x0, [x20, #IR_DEST]
    mov x21, x0

    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_ir_emit

.Lbc_done:
    mov x0, x21                  // return vreg

    ldr x23, [sp, #48]
    ldp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #64
    ret
@| _kcc_ir_build_cast
@}

